Great project to turn into a preprint — and smart to keep it strictly to what you actually ran. Here's a reviewer-serious plan built only from your synopsis + scratch notes:

```paper-plan
{
  "title": "What the optimal relaxation parameter really buys — classical iterative solvers on the 2D Poisson problem, predicted versus measured",
  "style": "arxiv",
  "abstract_sketch": "We compare measured iteration counts to fixed relative-residual tolerance with textbook asymptotic predictions for Jacobi, Gauss-Seidel, and SOR with optimal omega* on the 5-point discretization of -Delta u = 1 on the unit square with homogeneous Dirichlet conditions for n=16, 32, 64. Jacobi and Gauss-Seidel match predictions closely; SOR with omega* is dramatically faster but needs ~30% more iterations than the spectral-radius prediction.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "summary": "Motivation from synopsis: textbooks quote asymptotic spectral radii but rarely check against counts to fixed tolerance. Goal: short reproducible teaching reference on how far asymptotics can be trusted at moderate n."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Model Problem and Discretization",
      "summary": "Minus Laplacian with f=1 on unit square, homogeneous Dirichlet, 5-point stencil on n x n interior grid, h=1/(n+1). Sizes tested: n=16 (256), n=32 (1024), n=64 (4096) unknowns."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Methods",
      "summary": "Jacobi, Gauss-Seidel, and SOR with classical optimal parameter w* = 2/(1+sin(pi h)). State red-black ordering used for vectorization in numpy, with consistently-ordered claim to be verified against Young. Values used: w*=1.6895, 1.8264, 1.9078 for n=16,32,64."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Theoretical Predictions",
      "summary": "State formulas from notes only: rho_J = cos(pi h), rho_GS = rho_J^2, rho_SOR = w*-1. Predicted iterations ln(1e-8)/ln(rho): Jacobi 1073/4059/15765, GS 537/2030/7883, SOR 50/97/191."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Experimental Setup",
      "summary": "Stopping rule relative residual ||r_k||_2/||r_0||_2 < 1e-8 from u0=0, pure numpy implementation. Note n=128 not run — too slow — so no claim beyond n=64."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Results",
      "summary": "Measured iterations from terminal: n=16: 1064/543/65; n=32: 4020/2048/129; n=64: 15599/7948/261 for Jacobi/GS/SOR. Table predicted vs measured. Proposed figure: log-log iterations vs n with slopes 2/2/1 as figure idea from notes."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Discussion",
      "summary": "Jacobi and GS match predictions nicely; SOR shows systematic gap (e.g. 65 vs 50, 129 vs 97, 261 vs 191). Report gap without explaining via untested non-normal transient — explicitly out of scope per notes."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Limitations and Future Work",
      "summary": "Limited to one RHS, one tolerance, moderate n, pure numpy. Future work note only: comparison with conjugate gradient mentioned in notes as out-of-scope idea."
    }
  ],
  "sources_needed": ["Verify Young (thesis 1950 + 1954 paper) for w* and red-black vs lexicographic same rate via consistent ordering", "Varga Matrix Iterative Analysis, Golub & Van Loan ch. 11, LeVeque FD book for model problem background"]
}
```

What I **deliberately left out** to keep this credible:
- No n=128 — your notes say it didn't run
- No claim about non-normal transient hump — you marked NOT tested
- No conjugate-gradient comparison — you marked out of scope
- None of the seminar/groceries/rent TODOs

If this plan looks good, say so and I'll write the full draft. Want any tweak to the emphasis — e.g. more teaching-focused vs. more theory-focused?

Perfect — keeping the SOR gap open is exactly what makes this credible.

I searched for SOR background, then actually fetched and read one freely-readable source in full: the Wikipedia overview page **Successive over-relaxation**. Here's the revised plan using only that for Related Work — nothing else cited as read:

