    },
    {
      "heading": "Theoretical Predictions",
      "summary": "State formulas from notes only: rho_J = cos(pi h), rho_GS = rho_J^2, rho_SOR = w*-1. Predicted iterations ln(1e-8)/ln(rho): Jacobi 1073/4059/15765, GS 537/2030/7883, SOR 50/97/191."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Experimental Setup",
      "summary": "Stopping rule relative residual ||r_k||_2/||r_0||_2 < 1e-8 from u0=0, pure numpy implementation. Note n=128 not run — too slow — so no claim beyond n=64."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Results",
      "summary": "Measured iterations from notes: n=16: 1064/543/65; n=32: 4020/2048/129; n=64: 15599/7948/261 for Jacobi/GS/SOR. Table predicted vs measured. Proposed figure: log-log iterations vs n with slopes 2/2/1 as figure idea from notes. Scaling: Jacobi and GS ~3.8-3.9x per doubling, SOR ~2x."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Discussion",
      "summary": "Jacobi and GS match predictions within a few percent, GS about half Jacobi. SOR much faster asymptotically but systematically above prediction (measured/predicted ~1.30, 1.33, 1.37). Report gap as open question only — do NOT claim non-normal transient explanation, omega sweep, or other untested cause per notes."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Limitations and Future Work",
      "summary": "Limited to one RHS, one tolerance, moderate n, pure numpy. Future work note only: comparison with conjugate gradient and multigrid mentioned in notes as out-of-scope ideas, plus n=128, sweep around w*, and transient test as explicitly not done."
    }
  ],
  "sources_needed": ["Wikipedia page 'Successive over-relaxation' — actually fetched and read, will be the only background citation", "Young thesis 1950 + 1954 paper, Varga Matrix Iterative Analysis, Golub & Van Loan ch. 11, LeVeque FD book — listed in notes as refs to chase, NOT yet verified, do not cite as read"]
}
